    <fr:route>/thm-0003/</fr:route>
    <fr:title text="Existence theorem">Existence theorem</fr:title>
    <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    There exists an ordered field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> that satisfies the <fr:link href="/def-0012/" title="Least upper bound property" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0012/" display-uri="def-0012" type="local">least upper bound property</fr:link>.
    Moreover <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> contains <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex> as a subfield.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
  <fr:backmatter>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="References">References</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter />
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Context">Context</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2024</fr:year>
              <fr:month>1</fr:month>
              <fr:day>27</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/math-0004/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>math-0004</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/math-0004/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The Construction of \mathbb {R}">The Construction of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex></fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Math Analysis</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>
    We start constructing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex> by a way that it satisfies the existence theorem,
    the core of construction.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-0003/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>thm-0003</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/thm-0003/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Existence theorem">Existence theorem</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    There exists an ordered field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> that satisfies the <fr:link href="/def-0012/" title="Least upper bound property" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0012/" display-uri="def-0012" type="local">least upper bound property</fr:link>.
    Moreover <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> contains <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex> as a subfield.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    The least-upper-bound property mentioned above is defined:
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0012/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-0012</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-0012/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Least upper bound property">Least upper bound property</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> has the least upper bound property if every non-empty subset <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[T]]></fr:tex> of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> that is bounded above has a least upper bound <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sup  T]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Why do we need the least-upper-bound property?
    Consider the set <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S = \{x \in  \mathbb {Q} | x^2 < 2\}]]></fr:tex>.
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> is bounded above by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2]]></fr:tex>, but it does not have a least upper bound in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex>.
    Therefore we can't express <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sqrt {2}]]></fr:tex> in field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex> since some "gaps" exist.
    This fact motivates us to construct a more complete field <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex>.
    We have constructed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Z}]]></fr:tex>, and now we construct <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> from <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex>.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    Then we should find a way to express "<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sqrt {2}]]></fr:tex>" using <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex>.
    A crucial idea is <html:strong>approximating</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sqrt {2}]]></fr:tex> by a sequence of rational numbers.
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \sqrt {2} := \{ p^2<2 \lor  p<0, p\in \mathbb {Q} \}
    ]]></fr:tex>
    We can cut the number axis into two pieces by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sqrt {2}]]></fr:tex>, such cut is called a <html:strong>Dedekind cut</html:strong>. 
    A cut should be well-defined rather than just an intuitive concept.
</html:p>
            <html:p>
    As we use set theory to construct <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex>, it motivates us to define Dedekind cut as a set.
    It should satisfies some properties:
    <html:ul><html:li>Can't be empty or the whole <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>Closed downward</html:li>
        <html:li>Contains not the largest number</html:li></html:ul>
    A formal definition is given below:
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0013/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-0013</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-0013/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Dedekind cuts">Dedekind cuts</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    A Dedekind cut is a partition of the rationals <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex> into two non-empty sets <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[R]]></fr:tex> such that:
    <html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[L\neq \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[R\neq \emptyset ]]></fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x,y\in \mathbb {Q}, x<y]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[y\in  L]]></fr:tex> then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x\in  L]]></fr:tex></html:li>
        <html:li>if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p\in  L]]></fr:tex> then exists <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q\in  L]]></fr:tex> such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p<q]]></fr:tex></html:li></html:ul></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Now we can defined the real number as a set of Dedekind cuts.
</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/def-0014/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>def-0014</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/def-0014/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Real Number System">Real Number System</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The element of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> is a <fr:link href="/def-0013/" title="Dedekind cuts" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-0013/" display-uri="def-0013" type="local">Dedekind Cut</fr:link> in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {Q}]]></fr:tex>.
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        \mathbb {R} := \{ L | (L,R) \text { is a Dedekind Cut} \}
    ]]></fr:tex></html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <html:p>
    Now define the order relation on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex>.
    We have defined <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> as the set of Dedekind cuts, so we can define the strict partial order relation <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[<]]></fr:tex> on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> by the set operation <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\subset ]]></fr:tex>.
    The irreflexive, asymmetric and transitive properties are trivial. 
</html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
    <fr:tree show-metadata="false" hidden-when-empty="true">
      <fr:frontmatter>
        <fr:authors />
        <fr:title text="Backlinks">Backlinks</fr:title>
      </fr:frontmatter>
      <fr:mainmatter>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-001P/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>thm-001P</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/thm-001P/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Closed Intervals in the Real Line Are Compact">Closed Intervals in the Real Line Are Compact</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>
    For real numbers <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a\leq  b]]></fr:tex>, the closed interval
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,b]\subseteq \mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> is compact in the Euclidean topology.
</html:p>
 
   
   <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:taxon>Proof</fr:taxon></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
    The case <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a=b]]></fr:tex> is immediate. Assume <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a<b]]></fr:tex>, and let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {U}]]></fr:tex> be an
    open cover of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,b]]]></fr:tex>. Define
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        S=\{x\in [a,b]\mid  [a,x]\text { is covered by finitely many members of }
        \mathcal {U}\}.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    A member of the cover containing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a]]></fr:tex> also contains a short interval to
    the right of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a]]></fr:tex>, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> is nonempty. It is bounded above by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b]]></fr:tex>; let
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c=\sup  S]]></fr:tex>, using the <fr:link href="/thm-0003/" title="Existence theorem" uri="https://caimeo.space/thm-0003/" display-uri="thm-0003" type="local">least-upper-bound property</fr:link>.

    Suppose <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c<b]]></fr:tex>. Choose <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U\in \mathcal {U}]]></fr:tex> containing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex>. Since <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> is
    open in the subspace <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,b]]]></fr:tex>, some <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\varepsilon >0]]></fr:tex> satisfies
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        (c-\varepsilon ,c+\varepsilon )\cap [a,b]\subseteq  U.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    Choose <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x\in  S]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c-\frac {\varepsilon }{2}<x\leq  c]]></fr:tex>. A finite cover of
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,x]]]></fr:tex>, together with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>, covers <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,y]]]></fr:tex> for some <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[y>c]]></fr:tex>. This places
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[y]]></fr:tex> in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex>, contradicting that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> is an upper bound. Hence <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c=b]]></fr:tex>.

    Finally choose a member of the cover containing <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b]]></fr:tex>. For some
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\delta >0]]></fr:tex>, it contains every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x\in [a,b]]]></fr:tex> satisfying
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b-\delta <x\leq  b]]></fr:tex>. Since <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b=\sup  S]]></fr:tex>, choose
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[x\in  S]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[b-\delta <x\leq  b]]></fr:tex>. A finite cover of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,x]]]></fr:tex> together
    with this last member covers all of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[a,b]]]></fr:tex>. Therefore every open cover
    has a finite subcover.
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
 
</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://caimeo.space/thm-001O/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>thm-001O</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/thm-001O/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Intervals in the Real Line Are Connected">Intervals in the Real Line Are Connected</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>Theorem</fr:taxon>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter><html:p>
    Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[I\subseteq \mathbb {R}]]></fr:tex> be an interval: whenever <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a,b\in  I]]></fr:tex> and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[a<c<b]]></fr:tex>, one also has <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c\in  I]]></fr:tex>. Then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[I]]></fr:tex>, with the Euclidean subspace
    topology, is <fr:link href="/def-005Q/" title="Connected Space" uri="https://caimeo.space/def-005Q/" display-uri="def-005Q" type="local">connected</fr:link>.
</html:p>
 
   
   <fr:tree show-metadata="false" toc="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors /><fr:taxon>Proof</fr:taxon></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
    Suppose instead that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[I=U\cup  V]]></fr:tex> is a separation. Choose <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u\in  U]]></fr:tex> and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v\in  V]]></fr:tex>; after exchanging the names if necessary, assume <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u<v]]></fr:tex>. The
    interval property gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[u,v]\subseteq  I]]></fr:tex>. Set
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        S=U\cap [u,v],
        \qquad  c=\sup  S.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    The supremum exists by the <fr:link href="/thm-0003/" title="Existence theorem" uri="https://caimeo.space/thm-0003/" display-uri="thm-0003" type="local">least-upper-bound property</fr:link>, and
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[u\leq  c\leq  v]]></fr:tex>, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c\in  I]]></fr:tex>.

    The point <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex> cannot lie in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>. If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c<v]]></fr:tex>, openness of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[I]]></fr:tex>
    would place points of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U\cap [u,v]]]></fr:tex> strictly above <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c]]></fr:tex>, contradicting the
    definition of the supremum. If <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c=v]]></fr:tex>, then membership in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex> would
    contradict <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[v\in  V]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U\cap  V=\varnothing ]]></fr:tex>. Hence <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c\in  V]]></fr:tex>.

    Since <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V]]></fr:tex> is open in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[I]]></fr:tex>, some <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\varepsilon >0]]></fr:tex> satisfies
    <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
        (c-\varepsilon ,c+\varepsilon )\cap  I\subseteq  V.
    ]]></fr:tex>
    By the defining property of the supremum, there is some <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[s\in  S]]></fr:tex> with
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c-\varepsilon <s\leq  c]]></fr:tex>. Since <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c\notin  U]]></fr:tex>, in fact <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[s<c]]></fr:tex>. But then
    <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[s\in  U\cap  V]]></fr:tex>, a contradiction. Thus no separation exists.
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
 
</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
